    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/viriathus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/viriathus.jpg" alt="Viriathus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/viriathus">Viriathus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Viriato.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Eduardo Barrón, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lusitania took its name from the Lusitanians, a people of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> who had resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> for much of the second century BCE, most famously under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viriathus">Viriathus</a>, until his murder in 139 BCE. Their lands formed part of the Republican province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania-ulterior">Hispania Ulterior</a>, and campaigns by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decimus-junius-brutus-callaicus">Decimus Junius Brutus</a> in 138–137 BCE, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> as governor in 61–60 BCE and by others completed the conquest, interrupted by the war of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-sertorius">Sertorius</a> in the 70s BCE. When Augustus reorganised the provinces in 27 BCE he began dividing Ulterior into Baetica in the south, left to the Senate, and Lusitania in the west under his own control. The final arrangement may date only to his second stay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> in 16–13 BCE, when the north-western lands of Gallaecia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturia">Asturia</a>, at first probably attached to Lusitania, were transferred to Tarraconensis. Thereafter Lusitania stretched from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Douro</a> in the north to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algarve">Algarve</a> coast in the south and eastwards into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meseta-central">Meseta</a>, taking in the lands of the Lusitanians, the Vettones around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avila">Ávila</a>, and the Celtici of the south. In 25 BCE the legate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-carisius">Publius Carisius</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerita-augusta">Emerita Augusta</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadiana">Guadiana</a> for veterans discharged after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantabrian-wars">Cantabrian Wars</a>, and the new colony became the provincial capital.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/setubal"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/setubal.jpg" alt="Setúbal" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/setubal">Setúbal</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Setubal_III_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Filipe Rocha,user:sacavem (pt) http://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Usu%C3%A1rio:Sacavem (pt:usuário:sacavem), CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lusitania was a predominantly rural province of farms, pastures and mines. The valleys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadiana">Guadiana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Douro</a> produced grain, wine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, the uplands supported sheep, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and the Lusitanian horses that Roman writers praised for their speed, and the coast supported fishing and the salting of fish. At Tróia, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/setubal">Setúbal</a>, archaeologists have excavated one of the largest fish-salting complexes known in the Roman world, producing salted fish and sauces such as <em>garum</em> for export in locally made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amphora">amphorae</a>. Mining was a major source of wealth: bronze tablets found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vipasca">Vipasca</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aljustrel">Aljustrel</a>) preserve imperial regulations, probably of Hadrian's reign, for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines and for the life of the mining community, covering the baths, cobblers, barbers and schoolmasters. Country estates (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/villa-estate">villae</a></em>) multiplied from the second century and flourished in the fourth, as at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-cucufate">São Cucufate</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torre-de-palma">Torre de Palma</a> with their mosaics. Towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Olisipo</a> (Lisbon), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pax-julia">Pax Iulia</a> (Beja), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evora">Ebora</a> (Évora), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conimbriga">Conimbriga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santarem">Scallabis</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santarem-portugal">Santarém</a>) adopted Roman urban life, while in the interior native languages and cults, such as that of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/endovelicus">Endovelicus</a>, survived alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Roman religion. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> spread early: a letter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprian">Cyprian of Carthage</a> shows that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerita-augusta">Emerita</a> had a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> by the mid-third century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cantabrian-wars"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cantabrian-wars.jpg" alt="Cantabrian Wars" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cantabrian-wars">Cantabrian Wars</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cantabrian_Wars.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LLs, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lusitania was an unarmed province for most of its history. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantabrian-wars">Cantabrian Wars</a> ended in 19 BCE the legions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> were concentrated in the north-west of Tarraconensis, and Lusitania's governors commanded at most small detachments and local forces; the veterans settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerita-augusta">Emerita Augusta</a> formed a body of loyal citizens rather than a garrison. This peace followed hard fighting. The Lusitanian War of 155–139 BCE, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viriathus">Viriathus</a> defeated several Roman armies before he was murdered by his own envoys, bribed by the Roman commander, made the Lusitanians a byword for resistance, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-sertorius">Quintus Sertorius</a> drew on Lusitanian support when he defied the government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from 80 to 72 BCE. Under the empire the main threats came from outside. In the 170s CE Moorish raiders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> crossed into southern Hispania, chiefly Baetica, and in the late third and fourth centuries towns such as Emerita and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conimbriga">Conimbriga</a> built or strengthened their walls; at Conimbriga the new circuit cut through the old town. Lusitanians also served in the Roman army far from home, in auxiliary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohorts</a> named after them on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> frontier and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>, Alans and Suebi entered Hispania in 409, the province had no army capable of resisting them and depended on Roman forces and allies sent from outside.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/otho"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otho.jpg" alt="Otho" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/otho">Otho</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_the_emperor_Otho,_Ma_1215_(cropped1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fred Romero from Paris, France, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lusitania was an imperial province, governed by a legate of the emperor (<em>legatus Augusti pro praetore</em>) of praetorian rank, with a procurator responsible for imperial revenues. Its best-known governor was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otho">Marcus Salvius Otho</a>, sent to Lusitania in 58 CE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>, who had taken Otho's wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poppaea-sabina">Poppaea Sabina</a>; Otho governed for a decade with a reputation for moderation, supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galba">Galba</a>'s revolt in 68 and briefly became emperor in 69. For justice and administration the province was divided into three assize districts (<em>conventus</em>), based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerita-augusta">Emerita</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pax-julia">Pax Iulia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santarem">Scallabis</a>. Local government lay with the towns: Roman colonies such as Emerita, Pax Iulia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caceres">Norba Caesarina</a> (Cáceres), municipalities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Olisipo</a>, and many native communities, which gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> rights when Vespasian extended them to all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> around 73–74 CE. A provincial council met at Emerita to conduct the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the emperor, whose temples and monuments dominated the city's forums. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>'s reforms, around 300, Lusitania became one of the provinces of the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocese-of-hispania">Diocese of the Spains</a>, governed by a <em>praeses</em> and later by a consular governor, and Emerita probably served as the residence of the diocesan vicar, making it the administrative capital of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> in the fourth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-diana-merida"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temple-of-diana-merida.jpg" alt="Temple of Diana (Mérida)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-diana-merida">Temple of Diana (Mérida)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_of_Diana_in_M%C3%A9rida.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fernando, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lusitania's greatest legacy is the Roman city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerita-augusta">Emerita Augusta</a>, today Mérida, whose monuments are among the best preserved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and have formed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1993. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, dedicated under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-vipsanius-agrippa">Marcus Agrippa</a> around 16–15 BCE, its amphitheatre, its circus, the so-called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-diana-merida">Temple of Diana</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct-of-los-milagros">Los Milagros</a> aqueduct fed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proserpina-dam">Proserpina</a> dam, and the bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadiana">Guadiana</a>, nearly 800 metres long, show the scale of Roman investment in the provincial capital. Elsewhere the bridge at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcantara-bridge">Alcántara</a> over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a>, built under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> around 104–106 by the architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-julius-lacer">Gaius Julius Lacer</a> and paid for by Lusitanian communities named on its inscription, still carries traffic, while the temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evora">Évora</a> and the ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conimbriga">Conimbriga</a> are major monuments of Portugal. Roman rule brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, from which Portuguese developed, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a>, roads and towns that remain the centres of the region today. The Christian community of Emerita produced the martyr <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eulalia-of-merida">Eulalia</a>, whose cult, celebrated by the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prudentius">Prudentius</a>, spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>. The province's name lived on. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> humanists identified Portugal with Lusitania, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-de-camoes">Luís de Camões</a> called his national epic of 1572 <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/os-lusiadas">Os Lusíadas</a></em>, ‘the Lusiads’, after the mythical Lusus; ‘Lusophone’ still describes the Portuguese-speaking world.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rechila"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rechila.jpg" alt="Rechila" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rechila">Rechila</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rechila.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alexander Vigo, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Roman rule in Lusitania ended amid the barbarian invasions of the fifth century. In the autumn of 409 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>, Alans and Suebi, who had crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in 406, broke through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, and after two years of plunder they divided the Iberian provinces among themselves by lot in 411: according to the chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hydatius">Hydatius</a>, the Alans took Lusitania and Carthaginiensis. In 416–418 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a>, fighting as Roman allies, destroyed the Alans' power and killed their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addac">Addax</a>, and the survivors joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdingi">Hasding Vandals</a>. Roman authority briefly revived, but in 429, as the Vandals under Gaiseric prepared to cross to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, they defeated the Suebic king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermigar">Hermigar</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerita-augusta">Emerita</a>, and Hermigar drowned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadiana">Guadiana</a>. The Suebi, now the main barbarian power in the west, began to expand from Gallaecia. In 439 their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rechila">Rechila</a> entered Emerita, and the following year he forced the Roman commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/censorius">Censorius</a> to surrender at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mertola">Myrtilis</a> (Mértola), effectively ending imperial control. Emerita served for a time as a Suebic royal centre and then, after the Visigoths defeated the Suebi in 456, became one of the leading cities of the Visigothic Kingdom. Lusitania survived as an administrative and church province, with Emerita its metropolitan see, until the Muslim conquest of 711–713.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Lusitania, the Roman province of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerita-augusta">Emerita Augusta</a>, brought Roman towns, roads, mining and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> language to the lands of modern Portugal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/extremadura">Extremadura</a> for over four centuries, leaving monuments such as Mérida and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcantara-bridge">Alcántara</a> and giving Portugal the classical name it still uses for itself.</em></p>

